				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">Proud with the guilds recent accomplishment of slaying the evil goddess, Terris Thule, Wraug had asked Trefellgion to severe the head free from the corpse so that he may have it preserved and mounted as a trophy within the great den of their guild’s tree house. Unfortunately, he had not given much thought to the size of the beast, nor what it’s severed head was going to weigh. Even though he was easily capable to handling the added weight, Wraug was thrown off balance by the new load and became quite irritated at how the straps of his packs began to dig into his skin. A monk’s fighting ability is greatly effected by the amount and how he carries his added load. 
<br />

<br />
Wishing that Lady Raveena would have allowed him to enslave the evil dwarves that have somehow seduced her into believing that they are our noble ally, he would have had those HoB fools pack this head for him like the mules he knows they are.  Just the mere thought of seeing those midgets bound in irons made him grin slightly has he casually walked down the trail that led to the ancient book that would transport him to the Plane of Knowledge. 
<br />

<br />
The Plane of Knowledge use to be a realm of deep study and inner discovery. Rumors say that it was the great magic users of the old times that had created this plane as a sanctuary for themselves to study all that is, so they could further increase their magical power. Since it’s recent discovery, this once quiet community that centered around the grand library has exploded into a central hub that caters to all walks of life as many adventures use this realm a secure way station for their travels.  
<br />

<br />
As Wraug entered the Plane of Knowledge, he attempted to walk tall and noble like as he heard all of the different gasps and comments from nearby individuals that noticed the trophy that was mounted high on his packs. He could smell the mouth watering food being prepared as he paraded past several of the merchant shops, but he was beginning to feel somewhat uncomfortable with all the newfound attention he was getting, so he decided that it was best if he got on his way sooner than later. 
<br />

<br />
Before making his final trek to the guild house, Wraug was in need of provisions. However, it was becoming quite difficult to make his way through the crowd of young adventurers that had been following him to gawk at his trophy. Unfortunately, the crowd turned their focus from gawking to begging for handouts from him, which in turn he had no patience for and rudely pushed through them as if they all did not exist. 
<br />

<br />
Wraug made his way to the magical stone that transported him to the great forest of elves. From here on, he quickened his pace to avoid the elves that lived in the woods. He had no ill will towards elves, and even found them to be quite attractive. However, it seems that most elves are followers of Tunare, and they have put a bounty upon his head for slaying their Goddess of Nature. 
<br />

<br />
Except for only one certain high elf enchantress that his heart longs for, Wraug doesn’t care much of what the elves think of him.  He is just thankful that Galadrena did not feel the same way towards him that her kin did. He would not be able to go on living if he thought for one moment that she had negative feelings towards him. With his thoughts on Galadrena, he begins to daydream and fantasize about her professing her undying love to him as he has nearly done to her on many occasions. Every time he has found the opportunity to be alone with her to express his desires, that annoying dwarven cleric that she has taken as a traveling companion appears to ruin the moment. There have been several instances where Wraug had the opportunity to secretly slay that foul creature and be rid of that thorn in his side for good. But the thought of how it would sadden his elven enchantress has stayed his hand every time. 
<br />

<br />
Wraug stops, and realizes that during all of his daydreaming that he has missed the hidden trail that leads to the guild’s tree house. Or at least he thinks he has…”Nothing here looks familiar”, he mumbled to himself. He then begins to wonder if he’s even in the right forest. Wraug has only been to the guild’s tree house twice during his adventures, and both of those times he was lead there in the darkness of night. “Bah…where’s a druid or ranger when ye need one”, he whispered under his breath. 
<br />

<br />
As Wraug was attempting to get his bearings, which is something that doesn’t come easy to a monk, he felt his nerves stand on edge as if he was being watched. For some reason, he became uneasy. Wraug didn’t know if it was due to the fact he was carrying a god’s severed head, or the stench that it was beginning to produce, but he began to panic. His first reaction was to flee, which he did by running full steam through the heavy timber. He wasn’t too concerned about getting lost for he knew he could wander out eventually, so he barreled thru as fast as he could. Unfortunately, due to the added uneven weight to his pack, the second tree he attempted to weave and dodge around as he was running threw him off balance. Wraug went flying threw a patch of brambles backwards while spinning sideways to come out the other side and be nearly decapitated by a low branch extending from a scrub oak.
<br />

<br />
Wraug felt his ears ring as he lay on the ground moaning quietly. The branch had busted him right between the eyes and took him clean off his feet. “I’ll never understand how those rangers and druids do it”, he mumbled. After a few moments of laying there and imagining what kind of fool he must have looked like, Wraug remembered as to why he was put in that position in the first place. He just laid there and held his feign death pose, as he listened to hear if he was being pursued. The only thing that could be heard was the usual noise of the forest. He convinced himself that nothing could have followed him through that without making as much noise as he did, so he decided to get up. 
<br />

<br />
Wraug stood up and dusted himself off and removed several bramble thorns from his flesh. He took a heavy drink of water from his flask to quench the thirst that arose in his throat. After a good stretch to work out the kinks in his neck, Wraug turned to head back the way he had came. As he turned, he jumped back and “yelped” similar to the sound that a hound would make as if being kicked across the room.  Wraug was completely taken by surprise, for he was nearly face to face with three rangers who all had their bows drawn on him, yet never made a sound.
<br />

<br />
Wraug knew right away that these were not the elven rangers that inhabited the forest for they would have attacked right away. Upon calming himself down, and wondering why they just stood their with there weapons drawn on him, he noticed that one was a fellow human and the other two were elves. Wraug relaxed a little when he noticed they all were members of the Wudan guild. Yet, he couldn’t understand why they would have their weapons drawn on him, nor why they would even care about his existence for he was well below their social class. Ah, but one of them was a female woodelf, and Wraug couldn’t help to let his eyes wander about her person. After spending a couple of seconds of letting his lustful desires control his inspection of the female elf, Wraug said, “What’s going on mates?” 
<br />

<br />
The human ranger spoke up and said, “Hold fast while we summon our Captain.” The three just stood there with their weapons drawn on the monk. Wraug attempted to lighten the situation with humor by saying, “What are ye mates doing…openly recruiting members with a forced draft?” The three rangers just stood there with cold stares as they guarded the monk. Wraug couldn’t help but to occasionally stare at the female ranger, and even tried to wink at her a couple times to get some sort of rise out of her, but she just remained cold and hard as the others. Wraug seemed to grow more relaxed as they maintained the staring contest. Whatever the issue, he was sure he could talk his way out of it, for he had the tongue of a bard when it came to matters of negotiation.
<br />

<br />
After what seemed like an eternity, one of the rangers announced that their Captain is approaching. When Wraug saw the captain appear from behind the rangers, he accidentally blurted out, “What? A mangy dwarf as a Captain?”  The dwarf looked at Wraug and growled, which caused the monk to grin no matter how hard he tried to fight it. 
<br />

<br />
“So, yer guild be de one that stole our prize?”, the evil midget exclaimed. 
<br />

<br />
“What are ye talking about dwarf?”, Wraug replied.
<br />

<br />
“Don’t deny what ye have done, de proof be there on yer pack ye foolish hooman!” the nasty dwarf shouted. 
<br />

<br />
Wraug growled, “We killed her! Not you, ya stupid dwarf! Thus her prize was ours for the taking! And I don’t take kindly to dwarves yellin at me, do it again and I shall bust yer teeth out!” 
<br />

<br />
“So, ye thinks ye can take me do’s ye monk?”, the dwarf asked while chuckling. Wraug was unsure of what angered him more, the dwarf’s arrogant attitude or the fact that the female ranger would respond and smile at the rantings of that midget instead of him. 
<br />

<br />
Fighting every muscle in his body to keep from lashing out and delivering his staff upside the dwarf’s wiry head, Wraug replied in a cool and controlled voice, “Listen here ye filthy little…um…what I mean to say is, with no disrespect…is that ye mates have smoked yerselves some bad hobbit weed. Yer accusations are ludicrous, and if ye think I am going to stand here and cower down to a dwarf…ye are highly mistaken!”
<br />

<br />
The dwarf, grinning through his gristly beard, said, “Our rangers have tracked ye down, I have seen de proof of yer crime upon yer person. Wudan is now at WAR with Covenant of the Wolf for all that your guild has done to us. Prepare to die monk!”
<br />

<br />
Upon hearing the words that spew from the fat stump before him, Wraug attempted to take the initiative to give the dwarven Captain of Wudan something to remember him by. As he sprang through the air towards the dwarf, Wraug felt the sharp burn as three arrows tore into the flesh of his torso causing him to go limp as he landed on his knees. He tried to raise his weapon as the dwarf slowly walked up to him, but he could not. The rangers all hit their mark deep and true. It hurt to breathe…the blood gurgling in the back of his throat made him want to spit and choke…he knew he was defeated, yet Wraug did not give the fat dwarf the pleasure of watching him flail about or give any sound of pain. 
<br />

<br />
The dwarf just looked at Wraug and laughed. He barked at the other three, “Leave him here to die a slow death, as such a dwarf hating fool deserves!” Wraug just sat there on his knees, with three long arrow shafts extruding out from his chest. He started to unlock the doors deep into his mind where he could hide from the pain and await his death. Unfortunately, he accidentally unlocked the door at the lowest fathoms of his mind where he had trapped the demon dwarf that has haunted his thoughts for the majority of his life. As he began to hear the shrieking howls of the demented Dogmar in his head, he started to wish for his death to come more quickly.
<br />

<br />
Finding that only hell had been unleashed once again in his mind, he continued to fight the pain and hold his rigid composer. He watched as the dwarf reached over him and cut the severed head of Terris Thule away from his pack. The short fat piece of goblin dung then waved the trophy in front of him and did a little dance that looked like a pregnant pig heading to the trough. The dwarf then stuffed the head into one of his own packs and walked off out of sight as the rangers followed him in single file. 
<br />

<br />
Once Wraug could no longer see the Wudan scum, he relaxed his body and slouched back, still sitting on his knees. He coughed and choked on the pool of blood that was building in his throat. Wraug silently vowed to himself, “The Wudan that I once respected is now dead to me…(cough)…they have no honor…(weeze)…they seem to make their own laws…(spit)…I shall never hold any of their member with high regard again.”
<br />

<br />
Wraug then saw something coming towards him from the trees in front of him, but due to his blurred vision he could not make out what it was. He thought for sure it was some wild beast that smelled his blood coming for a ready meal. Wraug closed his eyes waiting for the torture of having his limbs ripped apart, but then he felt something warm and soft upon his cheek. Upon opening his eyes, we was amazed to see the female wood-elven ranger kneeling before him caressing his cheek with the back of her hand. She then whispered, “It is not right or honorable to let you just sit out here and suffer like this, I am truly sorry and may Tunare forgive you.” She then unsheathed a dagger from her boot, and drove it deep into Wraug’s neck. 
<br />

<br />
The intense pain only lasted for a second…then darkness.
<br />

<br />
Wraug slowly opened his eyes to find that his God of Tranquility, Quellious, had blessed him and reincarnated him back at the grounds where his soul was bound. Upon giving thanks and praise to his god, Wraug quickly set off to inform his guild of the crime and lies that he had just experienced. As he was running, he couldn’t help but think of the compassion that the ranger had given him, “Hmm…perhaps there is still hope for some of Wudan after this plays out.”<div style="vertical-align: bottom"><br /><br /><br />_________________<br /><a href="http://www.magelo.com/eq_view_profile.html?num=934990" target="_blank" class="postlink">Wraug</a> ....67 Human Monk
<br />
Dwarf Hater &amp; Executioner
